<Project>

  <!--
    Applies to all seven projects in the solution.

    PROJECT.md has recorded "0 build warnings" for a long time, and by the time anyone measured it
    the tree had two: a CS8600 in the broadcast rule and an xUnit1031 blocking wait in the router
    swap test. Neither was noticed, and neither could be — a warning in a 26-second build scrolls
    past above the line that says the build succeeded, so the count only ever goes up and the claim
    only ever gets staler.

    A warning here is a claim that something is probably wrong. This project's whole argument is
    that "probably wrong" is worth acting on before it becomes an incident, so the build is where
    that gets enforced rather than a document that says it was.

    Deliberately not scoped to Release or to CI. A warning an operator's machine reports and a
    developer's does not is the same defect discovered later, by someone with less context.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      Restore-time warnings stay warnings. NU1701/NU1603 are already waived per-project for
      packages that resolve against an older framework; promoting the rest of the NU family to
      errors would turn a transient feed outage into a compile failure, which describes the network
      rather than the code.
    -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
